<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Chincha Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 CE – 1476 CE (approx.) · capital La Centinela</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A wealthy lordship of Peru's south coast whose merchants and seafarers traded spondylus shell and whose lord was carried in a litter alongside Atahualpa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Chincha valley on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> became the centre of a powerful lordship from around 1000, after the decline of Wari influence. Its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-centinela">La Centinela</a>, with adobe pyramids and plazas, dominated the valley.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chincha society was highly specialised, with tens of thousands of farmers, fishermen and merchants according to a 16th-century account. Its merchants traded by sea northwards to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Ecuador</a> for spondylus shell and inland by llama caravan for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from the highlands. Chincha's wealth earned it a privileged position under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>: at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a> in 1532, the lord of Chincha was the only lord besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> carried in a litter, and he was killed in the attack.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Chincha was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> around 1476 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> built a palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-centinela">La Centinela</a>. After the Spanish conquest the population collapsed from disease, falling to a small fraction within decades.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1000 CE – 1476 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:79.60%;width:9.47%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 CE – 1476 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>La Centinela</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Local cults, cult of Chinchaycamac</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chincha, Señorío de Chincha</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1238&amp;focus=chincha-kingdom" aria-label="Chincha Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2592 2679 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chincha Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2912 2935L3027 2937"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2912 2935h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2912 2935h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks La Centinela. <a href="/atlas?year=1238&amp;focus=chincha-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

